## Verification: HARD RULES, no exceptions
[Name of user] [is/is not] a developer and [can/cannot] catch a false "done" by eye.
These are the failures that actually keep happening on this project, treat each as a
blocker, not a reminder. Delete any rule that doesn't apply; don't leave an inert one.

1. **Never write "verified", "working", "fixed", "deployed" or "confirmed" without
   pasting the command output that proves it, in the same reply.** No output means say
   what you did and did not check. "The code looks right" is not verification.
2. **Committed is not shipped.** [Where you actually test the live thing, e.g. "User
   tests on the deployed URL. A change is done only after deploy AND confirming it's
   in the live bundle/output."]
3. **Deploy is [describe your real deploy process, how many services, what order, any
   step that's easy to forget].**
4. **[Your equivalent of "tsc is not visual verification"] is not [what it doesn't
   prove].** [What actually proves the thing is done, e.g. "Render the page and look
   before saying UI is done."]
5. **[Any gap between test environment and production, e.g. "A green suite cannot see
   the live DB" / "the staging config differs from prod"].** Check a real copy before
   trusting anything recently added.
6. **Run tests with [your required env var / setup step], or [what breaks if you
   don't].** Never run two test runs or two deploys at once, [why, if relevant, e.g.
   "they clobber each other"].
7. **If you did not check it, say so.** An honest gap is always cheaper than a wrong
   confirmation.

**Rule 1 is enforced by a Stop hook**, `.claude/hooks/verify-claims.py`. If a reply
claims something was deployed / tested / verified and no matching command ran anywhere
in the session, the turn is blocked and you are told which check is missing. Denials
("not yet deployed") and quoted words never trip it. Run the check or state the gap.
Do not reword the claim to slip past it.

## Models
Deeper model where a mistake is expensive, faster model where it is cheap to undo.

| Work | Model |
|------|-------|
| Building, editing, running, small fixes | Sonnet |
| Planning, phases, architecture, reviewing a finished phase, hard debugging | Opus |
| A problem both have already failed on more than once | Fable |

Say which model ran the session in the end-of-session recap. If a planning or review task
arrives on the fast model, say so rather than proceeding quietly.

